import json,pathlib
r='frontier-40-geometry-braids-rep-27';base=pathlib.Path('research');p=base/(r+'-batch-25.pages.json');a=json.loads(p.read_text());rows={i['id']:i for q in a for i in q['items']}
src={'title':'The Stacks Project, Resolution of Surfaces, Sections 54.8–54.9: complete source arguments with local prerequisite replacements','url':'https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/download/resolve.pdf'}
def add(id,title,deps,statement,proof,source=src,kind='lemma'):
 if id in rows:raise ValueError(id)
 x={'id':id,'kind':kind,'title':title,'deps':sorted(set(['def-axiom-of-choice','def-dependent-choice']+deps)),'statement':'Assume AC and DC. '+statement,'strategy':proof,'design_row':'AG-BIR-1','local_addition':True,'provenance':{'statement':'literature-derived','proof':'not-applicable' if kind=='definition' else 'ai-altered'},'sources':{'references':[source]},'dependency_level':0};idx=next(j for j,y in enumerate(a[0]['items']) if y['id']=='thm-resolution-of-normal-surface-singularities');a[0]['items'].insert(idx,x);rows[id]=x;return id
flat=add('lem-surface-flat-base-change-coherent-cohomology-by-cech','Flat base change for quasi-coherent surface cohomology by Čech',['thm-cech-computes-qc-cohomology-separated-scheme-affine-cover','thm-affine-quasi-coherent-equivalence','thm-affine-fibre-product-tensor-ring'],r'''For a quasi-compact separated scheme $X$ over a ring $A$, a quasi-coherent sheaf $F$ and a flat $A$-algebra $C$, the canonical maps $H^q(X,F)\otimes_AC\to H^q(X_C,F_C)$ are isomorphisms for every $q$. No flatness of $F$ over $A$ is required.''',r'''Choose a finite affine cover of X. Separatedness makes its finite intersections affine, so its Čech complex computes quasi-coherent cohomology by the named comparison theorem. Base-changing each affine intersection replaces its section module by its tensor with C, by the affine quasi-coherent equivalence and the fibre-product tensor formula. The finite Čech complex of the base change is therefore the original complex tensored with C. Flat tensoring preserves cycles, boundaries and their quotient, yielding the stated canonical isomorphisms. All identifications are restrictions and tensor maps, hence natural and compatible with cohomology boundary maps. The same argument for localization identifies higher-direct-image stalks at a base point with the cohomology over its local spectrum.''')
leray=add('lem-normal-surface-modification-leray-short-exact-sequence','The Leray sequence for normal surface modifications',['def-normal-surface-modification-and-normalized-point-blowup','lem-local-normal-surface-modification-dimension-and-projective-cohomology','lem-surface-modification-isomorphism-in-codimension-one','thm-proper-pushforward-coherent','thm-leray-spectral-sequence-for-sheaf-cohomology',flat],r'''Let $A$ be a normal local domain of dimension two in the field/complete-equicharacteristic finite-type class, and $X'\xrightarrow gX\to\operatorname{Spec}A$ normal integral modifications. Then $g_*\mathcal O_{X'}=\mathcal O_X$ and $H^1(X,\mathcal O_X)\to H^1(X',\mathcal O_{X'})$ is injective. If $X$ is projective over $A$, there is a natural exact sequence $0\to H^1(X,\mathcal O_X)\to H^1(X',\mathcal O_{X'})\to H^0(X,R^1g_*\mathcal O_{X'})\to0$. The last sheaf is coherent, supported on finitely many closed points and generated by global sections.''',r'''On each affine normal target chart, proper coherent pushforward gives a finite algebra contained in the common function field; its elements are integral over the chart and hence lie in that normal chart. This proves g*O=O. The degree-one Leray five-term sequence then supplies the injection, without requiring projectivity of either scheme. The codimension-one isomorphism helper says g is an isomorphism off finitely many closed points of X. R1g*O is coherent by proper coherent finiteness and vanishes on that isomorphism open. A coherent sheaf supported on finitely many closed points is a pushforward from a finite zero-dimensional closed subscheme: a power of its radical annihilator annihilates the sheaf, whose coordinate rings are Artinian. Such a subscheme is a finite disjoint union of affine local Artinian schemes, so its finite modules are generated by their global sections and have no higher cohomology. If X is projective its two-affine cover makes H2(X,O)=0, so the next term of the five-term sequence vanishes and gives the short exact sequence. Localization/flat Čech comparison identifies the stalk at a closed point x with H1 of the localized modification over O_X,x.''')
rational=add('def-rational-normal-surface-singularity-and-bounded-modification-h1','Rational normal surface singularities and bounded modification cohomology',['def-normal-surface-modification-and-normalized-point-blowup','lem-normalized-point-blowups-dominate-local-normal-surface-modifications',leray],r'''A normal two-dimensional Noetherian local domain $A$ essentially of finite type over a field or complete equicharacteristic local base defines a rational singularity if $H^1(Y,\mathcal O_Y)=0$ for every normal integral proper modification $Y\to\operatorname{Spec}A$. Bounded modification H1 means these modules have uniformly bounded $A$-length. In both definitions it suffices to test projective modifications, or terminal schemes of finite normalized point-blowup sequences.''',r'''Normalized-point domination supplies a projective normalized point-blowup model dominating any proper normal modification. The Leray injection embeds the latter H1 into the model H1. Its finite length follows from proper coherent finiteness and isomorphism off the closed point. Conversely the restricted test schemes are themselves normal proper modifications. Thus their vanishing or a uniform bound implies the all-modifications version, and the reverse implication is immediate.''',kind='definition')
radical=add('lem-normal-local-surface-radical-multiple-of-a-principal-divisor','Reduced Cartier multiples on a normal local surface',['lem-normal-domain-implies-s-two','cor-regular-quotient-cohen-macaulay-equivalence','thm-prime-filtration-of-a-finite-module','thm-existence-of-systems-of-parameters','thm-height-one-localisation-of-normal-noetherian-domain-is-dvr','lem-r-one-s-two-intersection-of-height-one-localisations','lem-ring-detected-at-associated-prime-localizations','thm-finiteness-of-associated-primes'],r'''If $A$ is a normal two-dimensional Noetherian local domain and $0\ne a\in\mathfrak m$, there is $c\in\mathfrak m$ such that $A/cA$ is reduced and $a\mid c^n$ for some positive integer $n$.''',r'''Let p1,...,pr be the finite height-one primes containing a. Begin with nonzero f in their intersection and complete f to a parameter pair (f,g). Since m^u⊂(f,g), for h∈m^(u+1) write h=αf+βg with α,β∈m; then (f+h,g)=(f,g). Hence all these perturbations have the same quotient length ℓ. Each Q=A/(f+h) is one-dimensional CM, with g regular. Its prime filtration gives χ(g,Q)=length(Q/gQ)=ℓ: the two-term multiplication-by-g Euler characteristic is additive by its exact homology sequence; zero-dimensional factors have equal kernel/cokernel length and contribute zero, and each one-dimensional prime factor contributes length((A/P)/g)≥1 because g is a nonzero parameter on it. Every minimal prime occurs in the filtration, by localizing that filtration at the prime. Thus Q has at most ℓ minimal primes. Choose h in m^(u+1)∩p1∩...∩pr maximizing that number and replace f by f+h. Let q1,...,qs be its height-one primes and mj=v_qj(f). For each j choose aj∈qj with valuation one and outside all other q's. Here is an explicit construction avoiding nonprime symbolic-power avoidance: choose bj∈qj with valuation one (the localized ideal generates its DVR maximal ideal), choose cj∈qj outside the other primes by finite prime avoidance, and choose dj∈∏_(l≠j)ql outside qj. If bj lies in some other prime, replace it by bj+cj^2 dj; at qj the added term has valuation at least two, while at any other prime containing bj it is nonzero because cj avoids that prime and dj need not avoid it — instead use the following finite correction: start with cj; if its qj-valuation exceeds one, choose dj in all other q's and outside qj and use cj^2+bj dj. At each other q, the first summand is a unit and the second vanishes; at qj the second has valuation one and the first has at least two. This gives the desired aj. Set h′=∏_(mj=1)aj^2 ∏_(mj>1)aj. Multiply it by an element of m^(u+1) outside all qj, obtained by finite prime avoidance. Then f+h′ has valuation one at every qj. Maximality of s excludes any new height-one prime. The quotient A/(f+h′) is CM, so its associated primes are these minimal primes; its associated localizations are fields because all valuations are one. Associated-prime detection makes it reduced. Put c=f+h′. Its divisor contains all primes of a with multiplicity one, so for n≥max v_pi(a), c^n/a belongs to every height-one localization and therefore to A by the normal intersection supplier.''',source={'title':'The Stacks Project, More on Algebra, radical-element and divides-radical proofs, with explicit prime-filtration bound','url':'https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/download/more-algebra.pdf'})
torsion=add('lem-normal-surface-modification-uniform-principal-torsion-bound','Uniform principal torsion bound for surface modification cohomology',['def-normal-surface-modification-and-normalized-point-blowup',radical,'lem-surface-finite-type-normalization-finite','lem-projective-normal-surface-modification-h1-injects-off-special-fibre',leray,'thm-proper-quasi-finite-is-finite','thm-long-exact-sequence-sheaf-cohomology'],r'''For a normal local surface domain $A$ in the permitted finite-type class and $0\ne a\in A$, the lengths of $H^1(X,\mathcal O_X)[a]$ are uniformly bounded over all normal projective modifications $X\to\operatorname{Spec}A$.''',r'''If a is a unit there is no torsion. Otherwise the preceding radical-multiple lemma gives a divisor c with A/c reduced and a|c^n. Kernels of successive multiplication by c give length(M[c^n])≤n length(M[c]); thus it suffices to bound c-torsion. Its normalization T is finite: take the finitely many reduced components of A/c and their finite normalizations, with their product in the total quotient ring. Since the punctured spectrum of the one-dimensional reduced local ring is its finitely many minimal points, T/(A/c) is a finite module supported at m and has finite length. Let Z be the Cartier divisor c=0 on X and Z′ the schematic closure of its punctured part. Z′ is reduced and has no vertical components, because it is the closure of a reduced open; each one-dimensional component dominates one of Spec(A/c)'s components. A positive-dimensional fibre over m would contain a whole one-dimensional component, impossible by dominance. Therefore Z′ is proper quasi-finite, hence finite; its algebra embeds into T. If a section s of O_Z restricts to zero on Z′, its boundary in H1(X,O) restricts to zero on the punctured preimage. The H1 injection makes that boundary zero, so s lifts from A. Its zero restriction to Z′ forces that lift to be zero in A/c because the latter embeds in the algebra of Z′; hence s=0. Thus H0(Z,O) injects into T, compatibly with A/c. The multiplication-by-c exact sequence identifies H1(X,O)[c] with H0(Z,O)/(A/c). Its length is bounded by length(T/(A/c)), uniformly in X. This gives the bound for a. The statements only use projective H1 injection, exactly as assumed; general proper modifications are handled by domination and the Leray injection when required.''')
trace=add('lem-normal-surface-trace-cokernel-dualizes-h1-and-bounds-it','Trace cokernels detect and bound normal surface H1',['lem-normal-projective-surface-dualizing-module-over-regular-local-base','lem-projective-normal-surface-grauert-riemenschneider-vanishing','lem-finite-length-duality-over-a-regular-local-base',torsion,'def-rational-normal-surface-singularity-and-bounded-modification-h1'],r'''Let $R$ be regular local of dimension two and $A$ a finite normal local $R$-domain in the permitted class. For a projective normal modification $X$, put $M=H^1(X,\mathcal O_X)$. There is a canonical exact sequence $0\to\Gamma(X,\omega_X)\xrightarrow{\operatorname{tr}}\omega_A\to\operatorname{Ext}_R^2(M,R)\to0$, and the last module has the same $A$-annihilator as $M$. If one fixed nonzero $d\in A$ annihilates every such trace cokernel, then $A$ has bounded modification H1. If $A$ is rational, the trace is an isomorphism and gives an adjoint evaluation $f^*\omega_A\to\omega_X$.''',r'''The structure-sheaf cohomology has H0=A, H1=M of finite length and no other positive groups. Its truncation triangle is A→C→M[-1]→A[1]. Apply RHom_R(-,R), using that A is finite free over R (normal surface CM and regular parameters), and that finite-length R-duality is concentrated at Ext². The normal-surface relative duality identifies the middle dual with RΓ(X,ω_X), and GR vanishing makes that complex concentrated at degree zero. The long exact cohomology sequence is therefore exactly the displayed short exact sequence, whose injection is the trace dual to A→C. The finite-length duality helper gives equality of annihilators. If d kills all cokernels it kills M; the uniform principal-torsion bound then bounds length(M), and normalized-point domination plus Leray injection extend the bound to all proper normal modifications. If A is rational, M=0 and the trace is an isomorphism. Its inverse identifies ω_A with global sections of ω_X, whose usual sheaf evaluation produces the adjoint pullback map. All identifications are actual unit/evaluation pairings over the fixed regular base, not merely abstract module isomorphisms.''')
regular=add('lem-regular-local-surface-is-rational-by-point-blowup-domination','Regular local surfaces have rational modification cohomology',['def-rational-normal-surface-singularity-and-bounded-modification-h1','lem-normalized-point-blowups-dominate-local-normal-surface-modifications','lem-blowup-point-pushforward-vanishing',leray],r'''A regular two-dimensional local domain in the permitted finite-type class defines a rational singularity.''',r'''Any proper normal modification is dominated by a normalized-point-blowup sequence. Since the base is regular these are ordinary regular point blowups. The published point-blowup pushforward theorem, including its arbitrary-Noetherian regular-center clause, makes their composite higher structure-sheaf direct images zero. Its H1 is zero because the initial base is affine. The Leray injection embeds the original modification's H1 into zero, proving rationality.''')
# Explicit deps needed by the radical correction.
rows[radical]['deps']=sorted(set(rows[radical]['deps']+['lem-finite-prime-avoidance']))
